.TH EXPORTFS 4
.SH NAME
exportfs \- serve this namespace as 9P on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B exportfs
[
.B -r
.I path
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Exportfs
answers 9P on descriptor 0, which is the stream
.IR listen (8)
hands a service: the far end of a TCP conversation another machine's
.IR srv (4)
or
.IR import (4)
dialled.
Every request becomes the call a program makes on its own files:
a walk is a
.BR stat ,
a read a
.BR pread ,
a directory read a listing with a
.B stat
per name, a create a
.BR create .
What it serves is what its own namespace holds from
.I path
down, the root by default, so a
.B bind
before it starts decides what a client sees.
That is the whole access model.
.PP
The service script
.B /lib/service/tcp564
is
.BR "exec exportfs -r /" .
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/exportfs/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR listen (8),
.IR srv (4),
.IR import (4)
.SH BUGS
Reads and writes are answered on the serve loop's own thread, so a read
that parks in this namespace parks the loop, and a flush that reaches it
waits behind the read.
Rename and setattr are refused.
There is no authentication yet; a client is whoever dialled.
